{"name":"Horned Lizard","hex":"#e8ead5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"horned lizard","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of some 14 species of lizard in the genus Phrynosoma, generally with wide toad-shaped bodies less than a foot long, noted for the horny structures on their heads."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horned_lizard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Horned lizard","blocks":[{"text":"Phrynosoma, whose members are known as the horned lizards, horny toads, or horntoads, is a genus of North American lizards and the type genus of the family Phrynosomatidae. Their common names refer directly to their horns or to their flattened, rounded bodies, and blunt snouts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Mountain_Horned_Lizard%2C_Tutuaca%2C_Chihuahua_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_411168.jpg/330px-Mountain_Horned_Lizard%2C_Tutuaca%2C_Chihuahua_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_411168.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horned_lizard","wikidata":"Q288720"},{"title":"Horn (anatomy)","blocks":[{"text":"A horn is a permanent pointed projection on the head of various animals that consists of a covering of keratin and other proteins surrounding a core of live bone. Horns are distinct from antlers, which are not permanent. \nIn mammals, true horns are found mainly among the ruminant artiodactyls, in the families Antilocapridae (pronghorn) and Bovidae. Cattle horns arise from subcutaneous connective tissue and later fuse to the underlying frontal bone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Male_impala_profile.jpg/330px-Male_impala_profile.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horn_(anatomy)","wikidata":"Q65284752"},{"title":"Lizard","blocks":[{"text":"Lizard is the common name used for all squamate reptiles that are not snakes, encompassing over 7,000 species. They have a cosmopolitan distribution, ranging across all continents except Antarctica, as well as most oceanic island chains. Extant lizard species vary in size from the 13.5 mm (0.53 in) nano-chameleon and the 14–18 mm (0.55–0.71 in) Jaragua dwarf gecko to the 3-meter-long Komodo dragon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg/330px-Hemidactylus_platyurus_%28Flat-tailed_House_Gecko%29_on_white_background%2C_focus_stacking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lizard","wikidata":"Q15879"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg/500px-Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg/1280px-Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg/500px-Phrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhrynosoma_mcallii_17229024.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Texas_Horned_Lizard%2C_Aguirre_Springs_Rd%2C_Las_Cruces%2C_NM_88011%2C_USA_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_178115678.jpg/500px-Texas_Horned_Lizard%2C_Aguirre_Springs_Rd%2C_Las_Cruces%2C_NM_88011%2C_USA_imported_from_iNaturalist_photo_178115678.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Texas_Horned_Lizard%2C_Aguirre_Springs_Rd%2C_Las_Cruces%2C_NM_88011%2C_USA_imported_from_iNaturalist_pho